  "final_message": "I added the new field names but haven't removed the old ones yet. A straight rename would break billing-service and analytics-ingest, which read `totalCents` and `placedAt` from `OrderPlaced` today. The project's architecture rules (ARCH-4) say renames have to go through deprecation for that reason.\n\n**What changed**\n- **`src/core/events.ts`:** `OrderPlaced` now includes `amountCents` and `createdAt` alongside `totalCents` and `placedAt`, with the same values. The old fields are marked `@deprecated` and point to the new names.\n- **`test/app.test.ts`:** the event tests now check that both the old and new fields are present.\n- I left `schemaVersion` at `3`. Adding fields doesn't break anything, and changing the version could trip consumers that check it strictly.\n\nAll 39 tests pass, and the dependency checks (`npm run arch`) find no new violations.\n\n**What I didn't change**\n- The `GET /orders/{id}` and `POST /orders` responses still return `placedAt` and `totalCents`. You only asked about the event, and web-storefront depends on those responses (ARCH-8).\n- The internal `Order.placedAt` field and the `totalCents()` helper are unchanged, since they don't affect anyone outside this service.\n\n**To finish the rename**\n1. Ask the billing-service and analytics-ingest owners to switch to `amountCents` and `createdAt`.\n2. Once both have deployed the switch, remove `totalCents` and `placedAt` from the event in a later change.\n\nIf you'd like, I can draft the deprecation notice for those two teams.",
